  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nMidday visible satellite imagery outlined stratus eroding across\nSW KS, from southwest to northeast. This process will accelerate\nsome the next few hours, as breezy south winds increase and\nmixing increases, but northeast zones near Hays will likely \nremain under a stratus overcast through sunset. Temperatures \nat 4 pm will range from near 60 northeast to mid 70s southwest.\nThe rain event has ended, and SW KS will now remain dry through\nThursday. South winds will keep the boundary layer mixed and\ntemperatures unseasonably mild tonight, in the 50s through\nsunrise. \n\nStrong warming is expected Wednesday, with models forecasting a\nnet warming of +5C at 850 mb, along with addition of full\nsunshine and a SWly downslope compression component.\nTemperatures will respond, with all locations warming easily\nthrough the 70s, with lower 80s in the preferred warmest\ndownslope locations, from the Red Hills to Medicine Lodge.\nLittle if any change Thursday - a fantastic fall day with\nafternoon temperatures in the 70s once again, with less wind. \nSW KS is expected to remain dry through sunrise Friday. \n\nA weakening Pacific tropical cyclone Vance is forecast to be\nnear the southern Gulf of California early Thursday. Vance is\nweak and is forecast to become weaker with time, but still \nremnant moisture and vorticity maxima from this system are \nconsistently forecast by models to become absorbed in the SWly \nmidlevel flow, and regenerate with another subtropical \ndisturbance near the Permian Basin of W TX early Friday. \nWidespread rain will spread north rapidly ahead of this system, \nwith NBM pops now in the likely to definite range (70-90%) \nFriday afternoon, with WPC QPF guidance in the 0.25-0.50 inch \nrange, just Friday afternoon alone, favoring the southern and \nsoutheast counties. NBM maintains a mention of thunder with this\nevent, but with a tropical environment, lapse rates and \ninstability will again be poor, limiting thunder, but locally \nheavy rainfall will be the primary risk. Friday night football \ngames will be wet, but beautiful weather is expected late \nSaturday - especially for Halloween standards - with a clearing\nsky and temperatures falling through the 60s and 50s Saturday \nevening. \n\nTransient shortwave ridging should keep SW KS unseasonably mild\nand mainly dry for most of daylight Sunday. Global models and\ntheir ensemble members are showing continuity, forecasting the\npolar and subtropical jets combining into a strong plains\ncyclone early next week. We have high confidence this storm will\nexist, that it will be highly dynamic with very strong wind\nfields, and areas of heavy rain are expected. Cold air also\nappears lacking, typical of an El Nino setup, such that most if\nnot all precipitation will take the form of rain. Primary impact\nfrom this system is expected to be high winds, 50-70 mph, with a\nhigh wind warning probably required at some point. One week out,\nthe major global models do show some timing agreement that \nintense north winds are most likely next Tuesday.",
